  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 933 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nExtended range model guidance suggests that a weak low-level ridge \nwill build northeastward into the local forecast area on Wednesday \nnight and Thursday, in the wake of a departing surface trough and \naxis of rain/thunderstorms. This (along with unamplified/westerly \nflow aloft of 35-45 knots) will support dry conditions, with \novernight lows falling into the l-m 50s Thursday morning, followed by\nafternoon highs in the l-m 70s. On Thursday night, large scale \nascent will strengthen considerably as a mid-level wave digs east- \nsoutheastward into the TN Valley in the flow around an upper low \nacross the Great Lakes. Although the tropospheric column will be far \ntoo dry to support any risk for precipitation, a minor increase in \ncloud cover aloft should accompany the passage of this system. At the\nsurface, a reinforcing cold front will spread southeastward in \nconjunction with the shortwave trough, with light northerly winds in \nthe low-levels (beneath NW flow aloft) expected to result in a \ncontinuation of dry weather from Friday-Saturday night. Early morning\nlows in the m30s-l40s can be expected on both Saturday/Sunday, with \nafternoon highs in the l-m 60s. Low chances for rain may return to \nparts of the region by Sunday afternoon, either as a result of a \nweakening clipper system diving into the Mid-MS Valley or a lower- \nlatitude disturbance shifting eastward along the northwest Gulf \nCoast. However, due to vastly different model solutions at this time \nrange, we will keep POPs in the 10-20% range.",
